  url = {https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2017nsf....1651464T},
  urldate = {2025-09-10},
  abstract = {The upper atmosphere within the mesosphere and lower thermosphere (MLT) regions (80 to 105 km) is continually populated with waves generated in the lower atmosphere by a variety of weather-related sources or with waves generated by auroral activity. This award would deploy to the Poker Flat rocket range located near Fairbanks, Alaska, a state-of-the-art meteor radar system that would make middle atmosphere wind measurements with good height resolution (1-2 km) and good temporal resolution (30 to 60 min). The application of this instrument is to study the dynamics of the Arctic MLT region continually day and night. These new wind observations would be integrated into a study that combines radar and lidar measurements, satellite measurements, and meteorological re-analyses, to understand the wave-driven circulation of the Arctic polar atmosphere. The satellite observations and meteorological re-analyses yield synoptic-scale measurements of the mesosphere, stratosphere and troposphere. The lidar observations yield high-resolution height profile temperature and density measurements that allow characterization of the existing planetary waves, tides, and gravity waves. The new radar would yield accurate MLT wind measurements that complete the characterization of these waves and would support modeling efforts aimed at improving the understanding of the wave-driven global circulation. The investigators would use or re-analyze the MERRA (lower atmosphere below 80 km) and SABER profile data (to cover higher altitudes for the range of 90 to 110 km) to characterize the synoptic stratospheric and lower mesospheric activities. The radar observations would help to understand the extent to which nitrogen compounds produced by energetic particle precipitation in the thermosphere contribute to the composition of the lower atmosphere by transport across the MLT region into the stratosphere and the ozone layer. The activity in this new award will support the education and training of students in science and engineering. The award would extend research infrastructure in the Arctic, especially at the Poker Flat Rocket Range, enhance international and national collaborations, and promote collaboration among observers and modelers. The research results will be integrated into the University of Alaska programs and disseminated through a variety of professional, educational, and outreach programs. Analyses based on temperature and pressure measurements and gradient winds are used to describe planetary wave activity in the stratosphere and lower mesosphere, and the circulation follows well-defined synoptic patterns of cyclones and anti-cyclones. However, direct wind measurements are increasingly important at higher altitudes where the amplitudes of shorter-period waves and tides are the largest component of the winds. The activity would provide new observations as well as a coherent framework for understanding these interactions. Project Outcomes: This project resulted in the establishment and operation of a meteor radar at Poker Flat Research Range near Fairbanks, Alaska that makes high-resolution wind measurements in the middle atmosphere (80 100 km). The new wind observations have been used in studies that combines data from multiple meteor radars, radar and lidar measurements, satellite measurements, and meteorological reanalysis, to understand the wave-driven circulation of the polar atmosphere. Two studies looked at the impact of sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) events on the evolution of waves in the Arctic mesosphere. One study showed that nonlinear interactions between a tide component and a Rossby normal mode can be explained using theoretical energy arguments. The second study showed lower atmospheric waves that propagate to the middle atmosphere are the major contributor to the semidiurnal tidal response following a SSW. Two radar-lidar studies looking at Noctilucent Clouds (NLCs), used the Poker Flat Meteor Radar to characterize the background winds in which the clouds exist. One of those studies showed that NLCs are transported from the northeast over Poker Flat by southwestward winds. Finally, one study using 13 globally distributed meteor radars identified volcanic caused gravity waves in the middle atmosphere winds. The meteor radar system has been established at Poker Flat Research Range, a rocket range operated by the University of Alaska Fairbanks. To date it has supported four graduate student thesis, three journal articles (one in press), and many conference presentations. This radar will continue support future rocket- and ground-based investigations in an academic environment that supports education and training of young scientists and engineers. Last Modified: 02/13/2025 Submitted by: DeniseThorsen},
  annotation = {ADS Bibcode: 2017nsf....1651464T}
}

